Z||000002||ezsurf||11-11-2000||06:59 PM||||I still need some specs on the 2.8L V-6 heads.  What book or publication would list the port diameter and port length from the mating surface of the intake manifold to the base of the valve seat?  Or, does anyone have a set of heads sitting around they could measure and send me the specs?||||reg||5
Z||000003||Fiero_Freak||11-11-2000||08:01 PM||||   I'm not sure how accurate the some of that would be.  There are also some venturies ?sp? that boost the intake velocity by 17% supposedly.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ezsurf||11-11-2000||08:13 PM||||If that's true, I don't know where they would be.  I completely rebuilt the engine last summer.  I didn't see any type of secondary venturi on the engine or in the maintenance book.  I wish I would have known I would need these specs then, I could have easily measured them.||||reg||1
Z||000005||2FAST4U||11-11-2000||08:28 PM||||The venturi that you are referring to is actually a vane in the intake port floor. according to Chevrolet it increases the intake port flow by 17% by helping to bend the air flow around the short side radius. Most people think that it is a restriction to air flow but it's not so don't remove it when porting.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ezsurf||11-11-2000||08:33 PM||||That is correct, the high output heads have this vane.  However, it is quite a different animal than a venturi.||||reg||1
